I stopped before any edit, because the brief's stop condition applies: the r3 plan doesn't say where lift reads protection classes on a first exclusive export. I changed no code, tests or fixtures, and ran no tests, kondo or cljstyle. The only file I wrote is the report at `collab/1791223000000-compiler-engineer-ucf-d9-v1-lift.findings.md`.

**Why it's a ruling.** No code defines the machine's `:yin.k/custody` map (r3 1.1 describes it but nothing builds it), and a first export has none anyway. So the lift has no input for any of the header values the version-1 body needs:
- the occurrence
- the arbitration `{identity, descriptor}`
- the origin
- the next-op-seq
- the set of enrolled streams, which `:unprotected-pending` needs

`export-task`'s `opts` only knows `:cursor-profiles`, and I didn't build the rest of D9 without the header.

**Recommendation in the report.** Pass all of these in one `header` argument to `prepare`, which stores it in the export record beside `:served`. Then `encode` stays a pure function of the record, and a D14 journal can store the record. The composition would supply the header after folding the ledger reader. An incomplete fold would be `:unsatisfied` before `prepare` is called. The report also lists two alternatives and four short questions for the ruling.

**Two concerns for the fixtures.** Regenerating the C4 accepted fixtures through a real lift needs real programs that produce each base. The current mutation paths, such as `[:yin.k/frames 1 :yin.k/pending]`, depend on frame order and may need re-checking against the regenerated bases. Op ids can't come from a real run until D11, so op-id fixtures would need the id set by hand on the wait entry.

Once the header channel is ruled on, the rest of D9 follows from the brief.
